    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but i'm having a hard time visualizing it. if i have a single wave that's all messy and irregular, how does the math actually \"find\" the hidden sine waves inside of it? is there a way to think about it that isn't just looking at formulas?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine shining a beam of light (representing a test frequency) onto your messy wave. If the light frequency matches one of the hidden components in your wave, the wave will resonate strongly with that light, creating a big \"match\" signal. The Fourier Transform does this for *every possible* frequency, essentially checking how well the input signal correlates with every pure sine wave. These correlation strengths are then compiled into the frequency domain spectrum.",
      "source": "target",
